WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.200
In this section, we're going to discuss the various types of cameras available in Unreal Engine.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:14.199
When dealing with cameras in Unreal, we typically refer to two specific types, Game Camera Actors and Cine Camera Actors.

3
00:00:14.199 --> 00:00:19.199
Game cameras are the most basic type of camera and have limited functionality.

4
00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:26.230
Game cameras are often used as the primary player camera in video games due to the lightweight nature of their design.

5
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:32.229
Game cameras have a more robust set of features, including sensor crop, film back, depth of field, and tracking.

6
00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:38.229
They are often used for high-end cinematics and projects that want to achieve a more filmic quality.

7
00:00:38.229 --> 00:00:43.229
It's important to note that while games typically use game cameras for the in-game player camera,

8
00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:48.229
utilizing cine cameras in sequences is perfectly acceptable and often the norm.

9
00:00:48.229 --> 00:00:57.229
Outside of the performance costs associated with cine cameras, there's nothing preventing you from using either game or cine cameras at runtime.

10
00:00:57.229 --> 00:01:05.230
Just note that cine cameras offer a more robust set of features over game cameras and carry a certain performance cost for those features.

11
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:10.230
You can access the Cine Camera Actor from the Place Actors tab in the editor.

12
00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:21.230
In the Cine Camera Actors details, you will find all the settings associated with look at, tracking, film back or sensor settings, lens settings, and post-processing settings.

13
00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:26.489
For the purposes of this class, you are free to experiment with any and all of these settings.

14
00:01:26.489 --> 00:01:32.489
One particular setting to take note of is the constrain aspect ratio.

15
00:01:32.489 --> 00:01:39.489
If this is turned on, the camera will respect the aspect ratio of the camera as opposed to the player's monitor resolution.

16
00:01:39.489 --> 00:01:49.489
If players are using a different aspect ratio for their monitor than that of the cine camera specifies, black bars will appear on the top or sides of the viewport.

17
00:01:49.489 --> 00:01:56.549
Disabled in this checkbox will allow the camera to adjust its aspect ratio to the ratio of the player's monitor.

18
00:01:56.549 --> 00:02:02.549
Be sure to test the sequence on the target hardware in the event of unwanted scaling or distortion.

19
00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:09.189
When placing cameras into a sequence, a camera cuts track is automatically created.

20
00:02:09.189 --> 00:02:14.189
This is how the sequence associates the camera with the final output.

21
00:02:14.189 --> 00:02:22.189
You can change the camera binding at any time by right-clicking on the camera cut in the timeline and selecting the camera binding ID.

22
00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:30.539
Note, you can have multiple cameras nested in a sequence and swap between them by changing the camera binding ID.

23
00:02:30.539 --> 00:02:38.539
A few other components worth mentioning are the Scene Capture Component 2D camera, which captures the scene and can output to a render target texture.

24
00:02:38.539 --> 00:02:45.539
V-Cam Actors, which is used to transmit camera data to a device such as an iPad through the Live Link plugin.

25
00:02:45.539 --> 00:02:51.539
And Lens Component, which is used to create calibrated lens files to match a real-world equivalent.
